1
00:00:05,210 --> 00:00:16,920
[tn]: So, we were discussing the other day about themes, which is commonly translated... well, it's... the Pali word is nimitta, which I usually use the term themes.

2
00:00:16,922 --> 00:00:17,330
[nm]: Right.

3
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:19,751
[tn]: But it's signs...

4
00:00:20,149 --> 00:00:24,444
[nm]: Signs and features, signs and tokens, characteristics...

5
00:00:25,458 --> 00:00:29,145
[tn]: So, we'll just use the English word themes.

6
00:00:30,353 --> 00:00:31,130
[nm]: Yeah, yeah...

7
00:00:31,378 --> 00:00:34,993
[nm]: It doesn't quite correspond to nimitta, but it's certainly related.

8
00:00:34,993 --> 00:00:35,240
[tn]: Yeah.

9
00:00:38,949 --> 00:00:49,652
[tn]: So, I'm interested in that idea of developing certain themes, so as to, you know, for calming the mind.

10
00:00:49,650 --> 00:00:53,751
[tn]: There's that, as the sutta says, develop samadhi nimitta.

11
00:00:54,235 --> 00:00:54,522
[nm]: Right.

12
00:00:54,530 --> 00:00:55,764
[tn]: The theme of composure.

13
00:00:55,870 --> 00:00:56,223
[nm]: Right.

14
00:00:56,230 --> 00:00:58,334
[tn]: In the morning, in the afternoon and at night.

15
00:00:59,437 --> 00:00:59,680
[nm]: Right.

16
00:01:00,458 --> 00:01:08,701
[tn]: So, you have to take a particular theme and develop it, maintain it, the whole day.

17
00:01:09,065 --> 00:01:09,353
[nm]: Yeah.

18
00:01:09,644 --> 00:01:14,680
[nm]: Well, in that sense, theme, it's a good word, good practice as well.

19
00:01:15,128 --> 00:01:19,728
[nm]: It's what I was referring in some previous talks as context as well.

20
00:01:20,633 --> 00:01:21,513
[tn]: Context, yeah...

21
00:01:21,511 --> 00:01:24,030
[nm]: Yeah. So, establish your mind upon a certain theme.

22
00:01:24,461 --> 00:01:28,576
[nm]: It doesn't mean you hold that theme as some sort of mental object.

23
00:01:28,915 --> 00:01:38,181
[nm]: It means, actually, you discern it as a... as a... as a base... as a basis, in form of a context, as a basis for your action.

24
00:01:39,703 --> 00:01:46,633
[nm]: So, you want to develop theme that was going to give the overall mood to your experience, or direction towards your experience.

25
00:01:47,730 --> 00:01:48,865
[nm]: So, if your theme...

26
00:01:48,870 --> 00:01:50,010
[tn]: Gives direction, I like it.

27
00:01:50,012 --> 00:01:50,625
[nm]: Yeah, exactly.

28
00:01:50,630 --> 00:01:51,221
[nm]: It directs.

29
00:01:51,343 --> 00:01:52,957
[nm]: That's what I mean: context.

30
00:01:53,037 --> 00:01:59,090
[nm]: Like, context already... Oh, so these actions are against the backdrop of a certain context.

31
00:01:59,715 --> 00:02:15,621
[nm]: So, if that context is context that's free from greed, free from lust and desire, free from aversion, free from distraction, means no particular actions would be able to partake in that nature that this context is free from.

32
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:18,972
[nm]: And that's why, that's where that responsibility... that's what I mean.

33
00:02:18,970 --> 00:02:21,334
[nm]: It's not a meditation object that you just sort of ponder on.

34
00:02:21,334 --> 00:02:26,810
[nm]: It's something you need to actively think about, establish it, and then it's effortless.

35
00:02:26,810 --> 00:02:28,311
[nm]: Then you only need to maintain it.

36
00:02:29,021 --> 00:02:32,473
[nm]: And that's the basic practice of mindfulness done correctly.

37
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:44,109
[tn]: Rather than doing things which you think are free from anger, free from lust, free from delusion, I'll do these certain techniques, these movements, I'll say...

38
00:02:44,101 --> 00:02:49,080
[nm]: Yeah, that's like, that's looking at the opposite direction of the context, of the theme.

39
00:02:49,341 --> 00:02:55,623
[nm]: You're looking, oh, if I go through these motions, I will somehow evoke the theme I'm supposed to have.

40
00:02:55,960 --> 00:03:00,206
[nm]: But the only way to truly establish a theme is to discern what that theme is.

41
00:03:00,762 --> 00:03:13,181
[nm]: So, by dwelling on the context, on the nimittas, the characteristics of the context, you get to familiarize yourself with the context is, and then you see that your particular behavior is under the influence of the context that you established.

42
00:03:13,442 --> 00:03:21,309
[nm]: So, if your context is context of constant careless sensuality, careless speech, means your actions will be reflecting that context.

43
00:03:21,583 --> 00:03:26,208
[nm]: It will be directed by the context, by the theme of carelessness, by the theme of lust and greed.

44
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:30,400
[nm]: But if you just hope to mechanically induce it, it won't work.

45
00:03:30,402 --> 00:03:34,004
[nm]: Because, again, all you're focusing on is the particular actions.

46
00:03:34,320 --> 00:03:45,069
[nm]: So, although you're softly spoken and moving very slowly, you're just emulating what you think should be coming out of the context of calmness and context of non-ill will.

47
00:03:45,564 --> 00:03:49,842
[nm]: But if you actually get the context, you don't need to waste any effort emulating it, because you have it.

48
00:03:49,990 --> 00:03:50,385
[tn]: Yeah.

49
00:03:50,390 --> 00:03:52,606
[nm]: Why would you be pretending to have it, when you have it?

50
00:03:52,976 --> 00:03:54,187
[nm]: You can't get the context.

51
00:03:54,185 --> 00:03:57,189
[nm]: And then, even if your actions don't necessarily

52
00:03:58,056 --> 00:04:05,341
[nm]: mirror what you thought you should have been acting like from the context of non-ill-will, you know there is no ill-will.

53
00:04:05,909 --> 00:04:07,917
[nm]: Because you discern the context of non-ill-will.

54
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:14,526
[nm]: You haven't just blindly emulated and went through the motions and techniques of non-ill-will.

55
00:04:16,393 --> 00:04:17,400
[nm]: Same with loving-kindness.

56
00:04:17,402 --> 00:04:18,223
[nm]: Same with anything.

57
00:04:18,223 --> 00:04:19,018
[nm]: Any, any, anything.

58
00:04:19,010 --> 00:04:22,225
[nm]: And that's why mindfulness, if done rightly, results in wisdom.

59
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:25,804
[nm]: Because, see, you have to discern the context.

60
00:04:26,341 --> 00:04:29,543
[nm]: You have to understand the context, the theme.

61
00:04:30,581 --> 00:04:31,543
[nm]: Same with jhanas.

62
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:32,543
[nm]: That's what the Buddha said if...

63
00:04:32,890 --> 00:04:46,534
[nm]: if a monk doesn't... if he doesn't dwell, if he doesn't invest effort in developing the theme of jhana, which means he already has discerned the theme, but now he needs to invest the effort and develop that theme,

64
00:04:47,082 --> 00:04:55,892
[nm]: If he doesn't do so, he's like a mountain cow that, you know, as soon as it steps on one stone, thinks about the next, and doesn't get stable on any, and falls in between.

65
00:04:57,757 --> 00:05:02,526
[nm]: So, even with jhanas, even with samadhi, with composure, you actually want to develop the theme of composure.

66
00:05:02,677 --> 00:05:04,300
[nm]: Discern it, and then develop it.

67
00:05:04,305 --> 00:05:09,446
[nm]: Which means, you mustn't overshoot the mark, nor must you undershoot it, so to speak.

68
00:05:17,140 --> 00:05:25,941
[tn]: So, for example, the developing a mind of loving-kindness, you could, you could take a course.

69
00:05:27,553 --> 00:05:28,073
[tn]: That's all right.

70
00:05:28,190 --> 00:05:28,766
[tn]: It's sunny?

71
00:05:29,286 --> 00:05:31,210
[nm]: Yeah, I think he's gonna come behind the branches soon.

72
00:05:32,604 --> 00:05:37,757
[tn]: So, developing a mind of loving-kindness, you could go on a course.

73
00:05:37,753 --> 00:05:39,294
[tn]: You think, what is a person...

74
00:05:39,901 --> 00:05:43,029
[tn]: look like, or what does a person with loving-kindness do?

75
00:05:43,330 --> 00:05:45,012
[tn]: Well, he's softly spoken.

76
00:05:45,410 --> 00:05:46,204
[tn]: People like him.

77
00:05:46,210 --> 00:05:46,983
[tn]: He speaks gently.

78
00:05:46,983 --> 00:05:48,282
[tn]: He's good in communication.

79
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:53,667
[tn]: So, then, you decide, I will take a communication course in non-violent communication.

80
00:05:53,820 --> 00:05:54,151
[tn]: Yeah.

81
00:05:54,160 --> 00:05:58,412
[tn]: So that I can develop this special mind of loving-kindness.

82
00:05:59,420 --> 00:06:07,336
[tn]: And so, you do this course, but failing to see that you... have ill will, you have... you don't have a mind of loving-kindness.

83
00:06:07,334 --> 00:06:19,324
[nm]: Even if you don't necessarily have ill-will, as long as you don't discern the phenomena of the theme of loving-kindness, means all you're going to be doing is focusing on the particular aspects of your behavior.

84
00:06:20,246 --> 00:06:24,113
[nm]: And they should be coming out of loving-kindness, not inducing loving-kindness.

85
00:06:24,111 --> 00:06:25,210
[nm]: It doesn't work that way.

86
00:06:25,964 --> 00:06:28,353
[nm]: So, yeah, if you dwell on someone's behavior,

87
00:06:30,090 --> 00:06:35,016
[nm]: and discern certain traits, so that you can understand the context behind, that's fine.

88
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:49,768
[nm]: But it's when people don't look behind, they don't look peripherally to see what gives meaning to these actions, they just hope, if I go through the motions of these actions, I will have the same result as this other person, that is seemingly acting out of loving-kindness.

89
00:06:51,882 --> 00:06:53,913
[nm]: And that's a... that's a wrong order of things.

90
00:06:55,901 --> 00:07:05,953
[nm]: And that's why those people will struggle then, reconciling like certain behaviors of an arahant that don't fit with their preconceived notion of how arahant should be mechanically acting.

91
00:07:06,446 --> 00:07:08,574
[nm]: And that's... that... those are the cases in the suttas.

92
00:07:09,300 --> 00:07:21,835
[nm]: When the Buddha said, no, no, like, you know, he might have been roughly, you know, speaking roughly and so on, but things that arahant can't do are things of sensual nature, things of ill will, but all the other things are just... can be also attributed to various habits.

93
00:07:23,254 --> 00:07:25,008
[nm]: And, and those are not the criteria.

94
00:07:25,210 --> 00:07:30,096
[nm]: So, the little, like, little ritual, little, little kind of patterns of your behavior.

95
00:07:30,090 --> 00:07:33,766
[nm]: And, if you're investing effort in that, means you don't know where the problem is.

96
00:07:34,650 --> 00:07:37,974
[nm]: The problem is you being bound by the context of ignorance.

97
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:43,410
[nm]: Bound by the context of lust, aversion, conceit, self-centeredness.

98
00:07:43,430 --> 00:07:46,187
[nm]: Bound by the context of upādāna, context of assumptions.

99
00:07:48,324 --> 00:07:58,465
[nm]: And because you don't see that, you think that refining your behavior and learning how to speak slowly and walk slowly, you're somehow contributing towards purification.

100
00:07:58,474 --> 00:07:58,794
[nm]: You're not.

101
00:07:58,930 --> 00:08:01,496
[nm]: You're actually trying to deal with the problem where problem isn't.

102
00:08:03,070 --> 00:08:10,669
[nm]: Like, speaking harshly and so on, are usually the symptoms of the problem of the context of ill-will.

103
00:08:11,730 --> 00:08:13,951
[nm]: But if you just keep refining the symptoms,

104
00:08:14,990 --> 00:08:20,345
[nm]: you all you do is basically refabricating the way that it will re-express itself.

105
00:08:20,515 --> 00:08:22,157
[nm]: It's going to find some other way of expressing.

106
00:08:22,585 --> 00:08:29,063
[nm]: As in, you know, imagine a person speaking very softly, but like fuming on the inside.

107
00:08:31,338 --> 00:08:38,218
[nm]: But if you actually... That's why the Buddha never talked about, like, you go around and sort of deal with this or deal with that.

108
00:08:38,300 --> 00:08:40,412
[nm]: He would always refer to uprooting the problem.

109
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:48,458
[nm]: So, you uproot it, then you don't have to deal with any of these little things and manifestations of it, because there's nothing fueling it, which means they cease to be the symptoms.

110
00:08:49,745 --> 00:08:50,147
[tn]: That's the thing.

111
00:08:50,149 --> 00:08:54,002
[tn]: You don't have to develop these physical skills.

112
00:08:55,362 --> 00:08:55,648
[nm]: No.

113
00:08:55,646 --> 00:09:03,288
[nm]: As far as development of physical skills goes, it's the vinaya, sīla, five precepts, eight precepts, ten precepts, whatever you're keeping.

114
00:09:03,686 --> 00:09:07,981
[nm]: That is the physical emulation of certain behavior.

115
00:09:08,263 --> 00:09:13,157
[nm]: Non-ill-will, non-sensuality, not lying, not stealing, not killing, not cheating, and so on.

116
00:09:13,720 --> 00:09:21,126
[nm]: But everything else, on top of that, will only be right if it's seen from the point of view of the right context, of the right theme.

117
00:09:25,246 --> 00:09:31,660
[tn]: And if you realize that the work is there in the mind, that you need to sort out...

118
00:09:31,661 --> 00:09:31,997
[nm]: Yes.

119
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:36,349
[tn]: ...cleaning the mind, a lot of... you know, you can save yourself a lot of time.

120
00:09:36,395 --> 00:09:39,277
[nm]: Oh, you can save a lot of time and a lot of effort as well.

121
00:09:39,968 --> 00:09:45,052
[nm]: And also, like, one of the reasons why practice is so difficult for people is because they don't know what to do.

122
00:09:46,070 --> 00:09:48,705
[nm]: Like, people deep down inside don't really know

123
00:09:50,189 --> 00:09:58,021
[nm]: what to do. So, they have faith, and then if I do this meditation, if I do this technique, if I do through these motions, that faith will result in something...

124
00:09:58,020 --> 00:10:07,322
[nm]: But deep down they don't see a connection between doing this, and wholesome, doing this, and unwholesome. Which means: they don't know skillful as skillful, unskillful as unskillful.

125
00:10:07,320 --> 00:10:10,957
[tn]: But isn't it the case that you need to know what you want?

126
00:10:11,650 --> 00:10:12,254
[nm]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

127
00:10:12,254 --> 00:10:12,500
[tn]: [?What's the point]?

128
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:13,256
[nm]: Yeah. That exactly.

129
00:10:13,452 --> 00:10:16,345
[nm]: How can you develop it if you don't know what you want, really?

130
00:10:16,341 --> 00:10:18,591
[nm]: If you don't know what you're supposed to develop, you can't develop it.

131
00:10:18,590 --> 00:10:19,050
[tn]: I think so.

132
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:22,176
[tn]: If you want a mind of loving-kindness, you need to have...

133
00:10:22,170 --> 00:10:23,707
[nm]: Understand what loving-kindness is.

134
00:10:23,701 --> 00:10:25,530
[tn]: You need to have a vivid image of it.

135
00:10:25,530 --> 00:10:30,393
[nm]: So, when a person, like the example you gave, when a person has the idea, I want to have a mind of loving-kindness,

136
00:10:30,751 --> 00:10:44,103
[nm]: Instead of taking their vague, ambiguous, not developed notion of loving-kindness for granted, and then just try and emulate what loving-kindness is in their mind, they should start questioning what they think loving-kindness is.

137
00:10:44,110 --> 00:10:45,149
[nm]: That's how you develop it.

138
00:10:45,150 --> 00:10:48,844
[nm]: You don't develop it by going through some set of magical performances and motions.

139
00:10:49,069 --> 00:10:50,911
[nm]: You develop it by understanding it.

140
00:10:51,200 --> 00:10:54,886
[nm]: By purifying your understanding, by clarifying your understanding.

141
00:10:55,360 --> 00:10:56,621
[nm]: So, if you ever thought: I want to be...

142
00:10:56,964 --> 00:10:58,530
[nm]: I want to have a mind of loving-kindness.

143
00:10:58,602 --> 00:11:02,120
[nm]: Oh, so I must speak slowly and smile and... No.

144
00:11:02,778 --> 00:11:04,246
[nm]: I want to have a mind of loving-kindness.

145
00:11:04,242 --> 00:11:06,056
[nm]: I must understand what loving-kindness is.

146
00:11:06,743 --> 00:11:09,035
[nm]: When I do, I will know how to protect that theme.

147
00:11:10,703 --> 00:11:12,160
[nm]: I will know how to protect that theme.

148
00:11:12,164 --> 00:11:15,532
[nm]: I will know how to advance it, how to develop it further.

149
00:11:16,265 --> 00:11:17,000
[nm]: That's protected.

150
00:11:17,181 --> 00:11:17,620
[nm]: Exactly.

151
00:11:17,953 --> 00:11:19,141
[nm]: And that's that verse.

152
00:11:19,766 --> 00:11:20,501
[nm]: Is it Loving Kindness?

153
00:11:20,501 --> 00:11:20,898
[nm]: It is.

154
00:11:20,906 --> 00:11:21,827
[nm]: It's in Loving Kindness Chant.

155
00:11:22,380 --> 00:11:25,650
[nm]: He protects his mind like a mother protects her only child.

156
00:11:26,974 --> 00:11:28,755
[tn]: To protect that higher mind.

157
00:11:28,750 --> 00:11:29,000
[nm]: Yes.

158
00:11:29,383 --> 00:11:33,710
[nm]: Protect that sign, protect that theme, the notion of the context of non-ill-will.

159
00:11:34,470 --> 00:11:35,806
[nm]: You start developing it, you protect it.

160
00:11:35,810 --> 00:11:42,006
[nm]: So you... which means, by developing the context, you already know what behavior is compatible with it, what behavior isn't.

161
00:11:42,372 --> 00:11:45,909
[tn]: But isn't the case that a theme is developed

162
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:54,858
[tn]: And how maintained, you don't have to really... you don't have to worry about your actions, so to speak, because that's where your actions are flowing from.

163
00:11:55,543 --> 00:11:56,050
[nm]: That's what I mean.

164
00:11:56,669 --> 00:12:07,389
[nm]: You will have confidence then, once you start discerning the thing correctly, practicing mindfulness rightly, you will have confidence to know, I don't need to police my behavior, as long as I police my mind.

165
00:12:07,460 --> 00:12:08,313
[tn]: Yeah, protect that.

166
00:12:08,357 --> 00:12:11,280
[nm]: Police the root of my mind means nothing bad can happen.

167
00:12:12,029 --> 00:12:13,412
[nm]: Nothing bad that I can do.

168
00:12:13,625 --> 00:12:14,180
[nm]: Impossible.

169
00:12:15,507 --> 00:12:16,115
[nm]: Impossible.

170
00:12:16,513 --> 00:12:26,688
[tn]: If a situation arises, a bad situation, someone steals something from you, but you've been protecting that mind of loving-kindness...

171
00:12:26,831 --> 00:12:27,065
[nm]: Yeah.

172
00:12:27,530 --> 00:12:33,907
[nm]: Means you don't abandon your context, because the priority of that which has been stolen from you is more important.

173
00:12:33,917 --> 00:12:34,132
[nm]: No.

174
00:12:34,587 --> 00:12:35,880
[nm]: The mind is the most important.

175
00:12:35,884 --> 00:12:39,027
[nm]: The context is the most important, which means everything else is second.

176
00:12:39,021 --> 00:12:40,581
[nm]: Which means whatever befalls you,

177
00:12:41,212 --> 00:12:46,160
[nm]: it will not give rise to lust, or aversion, or anything, because you still focus on protecting your mind.

178
00:12:46,164 --> 00:12:47,463
[tn]: It doesn't fit, as well.

179
00:12:47,461 --> 00:12:47,970
[nm]: Exactly.

180
00:12:47,970 --> 00:12:48,852
[nm]: It's not compatible.

181
00:12:48,852 --> 00:12:51,370
[tn]: [??] context... so it's not compatible.

182
00:12:51,372 --> 00:12:52,310
[nm]: It's not compatible with it.

183
00:12:52,842 --> 00:12:54,006
[tn]: So, it cannot come out of it.

184
00:12:54,058 --> 00:12:58,608
[nm]: So, you can... and you can, again, like any sutta, basically, every sutta is a theme in itself.

185
00:12:59,530 --> 00:13:00,040
[nm]: But that's what I mean.

186
00:13:00,412 --> 00:13:07,730
[nm]: Like, theme is not something you can just, just sort of... Well, you can particularize the theme of something.

187
00:13:08,830 --> 00:13:10,018
[nm]: But that particular...

188
00:13:10,886 --> 00:13:16,010
[nm]: those particular actions, particular things will have the meaning only on the basis of that general theme.

189
00:13:16,724 --> 00:13:17,870
[nm]: It's not other way around.

190
00:13:20,936 --> 00:13:28,158
[nm]: So, really, the practice would come down, instead of conceptualizing things, you should be contextualizing them.

191
00:13:29,246 --> 00:13:36,429
[nm]: You should be looking at the context behind your action, not behind what is on a molecular level, I'm moving my hand, moving my hand.

192
00:13:36,423 --> 00:13:36,684
[nm]: No.

193
00:13:37,568 --> 00:13:37,781
[nm]: No.

194
00:13:38,025 --> 00:13:39,172
[nm]: Why, why am I?

195
00:13:39,180 --> 00:13:40,014
[nm]: What is the reason?

196
00:13:40,020 --> 00:13:42,227
[nm]: What is my motivation for moving my hand?

197
00:13:42,705 --> 00:13:44,071
[nm]: Oh, I want more food.

198
00:13:44,395 --> 00:13:45,098
[nm]: Am I hungry?

199
00:13:46,298 --> 00:13:47,856
[nm]: Is it for desire and lust?

200
00:13:48,820 --> 00:13:49,882
[nm]: What is the purpose of it?

201
00:13:50,120 --> 00:13:51,414
[nm]: That's where you find the context.

202
00:13:51,420 --> 00:13:58,467
[nm]: Not in this like, like sort of fully focused absorption into one moment.

203
00:13:58,633 --> 00:13:58,875
[nm]: No.

204
00:13:59,080 --> 00:14:04,284
[nm]: That moment is already the result of the context that you're clearly not seeing because you're looking in the opposite direction.

205
00:14:06,021 --> 00:14:20,126
[nm]: So, the one-pointedness of mind is one-pointedness of the unchangeable context that induces, despite all these particular actions that are coming and going, not one-pointedness of I'm so one-pointed, no other points exist for me, which is usually how people take meditation to be.

206
00:14:21,109 --> 00:14:24,616
[nm]: But you could see, like, if you wonder, well, which one is better?

207
00:14:24,997 --> 00:14:27,928
[nm]: Well, ask yourself, which one is more likely to result in wisdom?

208
00:14:29,113 --> 00:14:30,063
[nm]: And what is wisdom?

209
00:14:31,254 --> 00:14:34,690
[nm]: Like, say you go to a wise man, or many wise men,

210
00:14:35,408 --> 00:14:36,831
[nm]: And what would be in common to all them?

211
00:14:36,830 --> 00:14:38,364
[nm]: Like you ask them with different problems.

212
00:14:38,370 --> 00:14:41,353
[nm]: What would be in common is that they have greater perspective.

213
00:14:42,652 --> 00:14:44,705
[nm]: That's where you go to ask for an advice.

214
00:14:45,924 --> 00:14:50,911
[nm]: Because you're gonna get a better perspective, because you are locked on to the particular problem you have.

215
00:14:52,762 --> 00:14:54,288
[nm]: And that's a definition of wisdom.

216
00:14:54,330 --> 00:14:55,549
[nm]: Development of perspective.

217
00:14:55,541 --> 00:14:58,500
[nm]: And what is perspective other than greater context?

218
00:14:59,465 --> 00:15:03,707
[nm]: You know, you have a more particular context, but then you have the fundamental universal context...

219
00:15:04,480 --> 00:15:11,096
[nm]: Of impermanence, context of non-ownership, context of suffering, context of not-self.

220
00:15:11,421 --> 00:15:17,568
[nm]: Fundamental, universal, included in everything possible that has a nature of being manifested.

221
00:15:17,863 --> 00:15:21,562
[nm]: It's as broad, as general, as universal as that.

222
00:15:22,098 --> 00:15:25,517
[nm]: So, developing the context means developing perspective on things.

223
00:15:26,271 --> 00:15:28,865
[nm]: Developing perspective is nothing other than developing wisdom.

224
00:15:30,670 --> 00:15:31,204
[nm]: And that's what I mean.

225
00:15:31,210 --> 00:15:40,473
[nm]: You ask yourself, so, will this practice of developing context, more likely result in wisdom, or practice of developing one-pointedness on a particular thing, and not give rise to anything else?

226
00:15:41,661 --> 00:15:43,391
[nm]: And you don't really need to look far for the answer.

227
00:15:43,391 --> 00:15:45,136
[nm]: It's already ordered, implied and obvious.

228
00:15:48,471 --> 00:15:50,016
[tn]: I might have said this before.

229
00:15:52,900 --> 00:15:56,654
[tn]: I mean, someone, people asking that, I want to become a monk, and so on.

230
00:15:58,553 --> 00:15:59,600
[tn]: And then, how should I do it?

231
00:15:59,620 --> 00:16:00,448
[tn]: Should I go about this?

232
00:16:00,450 --> 00:16:01,724
[tn]: And what's the best thing to do?

233
00:16:01,730 --> 00:16:04,040
[tn]: and, you know, where exactly should I do it?

234
00:16:04,151 --> 00:16:04,720
[tn]: And so on.

235
00:16:05,536 --> 00:16:12,831
[tn]: But, the thing is, you have to get that theme of renunciation.

236
00:16:13,911 --> 00:16:16,124
[tn]: Get that idea of renunciation and hold it there.

237
00:16:16,711 --> 00:16:18,381
[tn]: Don't do anything unwholesome.

238
00:16:18,930 --> 00:16:19,808
[tn]: Do what is wholesome.

239
00:16:20,555 --> 00:16:21,562
[tn]: And just do that.

240
00:16:22,160 --> 00:16:23,467
[tn]: Then it doesn't really matter...

241
00:16:24,060 --> 00:16:29,966
[tn]: You don't have to worry about which monastery should I go to, how should, where, you know, what country should I go to to practice.

242
00:16:30,450 --> 00:16:32,861
[tn]: You will be practicing and your life will...

243
00:16:32,863 --> 00:16:36,284
[nm]: You realize then that circumstances of your practice are exactly that.

244
00:16:36,282 --> 00:16:37,324
[nm]: Circumstantial.

245
00:16:38,164 --> 00:16:43,267
[nm]: Oh, they're contributing towards the context I'm trying to develop or they're a bit too incompatible.

246
00:16:43,776 --> 00:16:46,762
[nm]: You know, if you're like ordaining some crowded, constantly busy temple.

247
00:16:47,711 --> 00:16:53,728
[nm]: But that will become apparent if you'll be invested, like, clarifying the context of what you want to do, what you want to develop.

248
00:16:54,120 --> 00:16:59,014
[nm]: That's what Buddha meant, he goes and listens to wise men, he hears the advice, then he measures the task.

249
00:16:59,313 --> 00:17:00,378
[nm]: I said that many times.

250
00:17:00,900 --> 00:17:02,467
[nm]: That's something people always overlook.

251
00:17:03,016 --> 00:17:05,972
[nm]: Measuring the task means understanding what needs to be done.

252
00:17:06,804 --> 00:17:09,909
[nm]: And when you know what needs to be done, you will have inspiration to do it as well.

253
00:17:09,953 --> 00:17:12,151
[nm]: But most people don't really know what needs to be done.

254
00:17:13,058 --> 00:17:14,658
[nm]: So, they end up doing everything and nothing.

255
00:17:15,510 --> 00:17:18,094
[nm]: Because they have no criteria, as in what works, what doesn't.

256
00:17:20,310 --> 00:17:23,827
[nm]: But yes, even just being a monk, wearing a robe, it's already a context.

257
00:17:25,042 --> 00:17:28,235
[nm]: That's why back in the day, when the order started, they had no rules.

258
00:17:28,517 --> 00:17:29,244
[nm]: There were no rules.

259
00:17:29,835 --> 00:17:33,021
[nm]: Because everybody... Understanding dhamma means they understood the context.

260
00:17:33,750 --> 00:17:38,530
[nm]: Means they already had that basis of wisdom, which means they would not do anything incompatible.

261
00:17:39,886 --> 00:17:40,400
[nm]: So they were...

262
00:17:40,835 --> 00:17:45,014
[nm]: I mean, they were going pindapada at night, eating in the afternoon, because none of that was an issue.

263
00:17:45,820 --> 00:17:47,621
[nm]: But it's when context started declining...

264
00:17:48,073 --> 00:17:52,755
[nm]: So then, when new people would come and hear the dhamma, they wouldn't get the same level of context.

265
00:17:53,435 --> 00:17:55,343
[nm]: They wouldn't have the same perspective on it.

266
00:17:55,860 --> 00:18:02,762
[nm]: And then, they would do things that are directly contrary to the practice of dhamma, out of desire, out of aversion, out of distraction and delusion.

267
00:18:02,943 --> 00:18:05,406
[nm]: And that's where the rules started sort of evolving.

268
00:18:06,191 --> 00:18:07,892
[nm]: Okay, don't do this, don't do this.

269
00:18:08,300 --> 00:18:09,627
[nm]: But in the beginning, there was nothing.

270
00:18:10,069 --> 00:18:11,650
[nm]: Because there was no need for anything.

271
00:18:16,200 --> 00:18:20,793
[tn]: So, trust, that's what I wanted to say, as well, is trust in the context.

272
00:18:22,128 --> 00:18:28,978
[nm]: Yes, but you can only have trust in the context if you at least have a degree of some discernment of what the context is.

273
00:18:29,336 --> 00:18:36,570
[tn]: For example, like, if you trust in the context of sensuality, you can be guaranteed you'll be going down the sensual path.

274
00:18:36,570 --> 00:18:37,240
[tn]: Don't worry about it.

275
00:18:37,240 --> 00:18:38,075
[nm]: Sure, sure.

276
00:18:38,524 --> 00:18:42,585
[nm]: Yeah, or if... you don't even have to, like, define trust.

277
00:18:42,583 --> 00:18:45,138
[nm]: You can more like just welcome the context of sensuality.

278
00:18:45,730 --> 00:18:47,812
[nm]: Just not question the context of sensuality.

279
00:18:47,820 --> 00:18:48,848
[nm]: Take it for granted.

280
00:18:51,630 --> 00:18:58,284
[nm]: Because sensuality, ill-will, distraction are basically contexts that bring you... that go with the grain of your behavior.

281
00:18:59,027 --> 00:19:03,338
[nm]: In other words, for somebody who is affected with ignorance, those contexts are effortless.

282
00:19:03,341 --> 00:19:04,858
[nm]: Those contexts are default.

283
00:19:05,907 --> 00:19:13,126
[nm]: So, that's why initially you have to go against the grain and discern the greater context and start seeing the benefit in the context of restraint.

284
00:19:13,772 --> 00:19:16,820
[nm]: Context of non-ill-will, context of loving-kindness and so on.

285
00:19:17,027 --> 00:19:17,267
[tn]: Right.

286
00:19:17,385 --> 00:19:18,722
[tn]: So, those are default.

287
00:19:19,143 --> 00:19:19,875
[nm]: Those are default.

288
00:19:19,910 --> 00:19:21,004
[nm]: They're effortless.

289
00:19:21,103 --> 00:19:21,372
[tn]: Yeah.

290
00:19:21,501 --> 00:19:24,440
[nm]: So, if you don't have the right context, you're automatically in the wrong context.

291
00:19:24,914 --> 00:19:27,496
[tn]: But there is a context and you should be aware of it.

292
00:19:28,004 --> 00:19:28,810
[nm]: Absolutely.

293
00:19:28,823 --> 00:19:30,966
[nm]: That's why you're always responsible for your actions.

294
00:19:31,762 --> 00:19:38,033
[nm]: You always are... That's why the mind comes first, because the context is the basis for any action, any significance, any meaning within it.

295
00:19:44,340 --> 00:19:46,368
[tn]: So, again, on the context, the...

296
00:19:47,480 --> 00:19:48,414
[nm]: The theme of the context.

297
00:19:48,421 --> 00:19:49,364
[tn]: The theme of [?your context].

298
00:19:51,200 --> 00:20:01,061
[tn]: The example I gave the other day, when, as I was in the forest, what I needed, what I wanted to have was a chair.

299
00:20:02,071 --> 00:20:10,694
[tn]: So, I didn't have a specific chair in mind, you know, armrest, four legs or whatever.

300
00:20:10,692 --> 00:20:12,736
[tn]: I just wanted this general...

301
00:20:13,682 --> 00:20:16,625
[tn]: resting place, chair, something I could rest my back.

302
00:20:16,621 --> 00:20:22,454
[tn]: So, I had this image in my mind of a general chair, nothing specific.

303
00:20:24,115 --> 00:20:28,267
[tn]: And I have that image in the background, in my mind, just there.

304
00:20:28,267 --> 00:20:28,271
[tn]: And I have the intention that that's what I want.
[tn]: And I have that image in the background, in my mind, just there.

305
00:20:28,271 --> 00:20:31,174
[tn]: And I have the intention that that's what I want.

306
00:20:31,501 --> 00:20:33,067
[tn]: It's clear to me what I want.

307
00:20:33,360 --> 00:20:34,593
[tn]: I have the feel of the chair.

308
00:20:34,814 --> 00:20:37,991
[nm]: But you're not actively thinking chair and armrest.

309
00:20:37,990 --> 00:20:41,684
[nm]: No, you just had the purpose of the chair at the back of your mind.

310
00:20:41,690 --> 00:20:42,553
[tn]: Yeah, the theme...

311
00:20:42,560 --> 00:20:43,764
[tn]: You know, almost felt.

312
00:20:43,903 --> 00:20:44,189
[nm]: Yeah.

313
00:20:44,450 --> 00:20:52,120
[tn]: I don't... I'm not measuring now the places where I could sit and then leveling ground or which is the levelest ground, sit on, whatever.

314
00:20:54,404 --> 00:21:04,008
[tn]: I just have that con... that image, background image, that background sign, with the intention working and then...

315
00:21:04,290 --> 00:21:13,484
[tn]: My body is moving, because when there's an intention, I want chair, make chair, the body is moving, those eyes are scanning, I'm looking at everything...

316
00:21:13,480 --> 00:21:18,724
[nm]: That's how all the particular acts become intelligible, from the greater perspective of the context.

317
00:21:18,724 --> 00:21:21,138
[nm]: That's why the mind is the forerunner of all things.

318
00:21:21,688 --> 00:21:24,740
[nm]: No, because mind mystically emerges somewhere else down the horizon.

319
00:21:24,741 --> 00:21:33,063
[nm]: No, it's because right there and then, while you're thinking that, the significance of your thoughts, your particular actions, are already directed by the mind.

320
00:21:33,614 --> 00:21:39,338
[nm]: By the context. So, you can have particular context, practical context, context rooted in this, context rooted in that.

321
00:21:39,932 --> 00:21:44,402
[nm]: But, fundamentally, if you start developing wisdom, you're going to start discerning the universal context.

322
00:21:45,938 --> 00:21:49,800
[nm]: And that's... the most universal context is suffering and being freed from it.

323
00:21:49,800 --> 00:21:50,543
[nm]: That's your goal.

324
00:21:50,541 --> 00:21:51,317
[nm]: That's your purpose.

325
00:21:51,313 --> 00:21:52,913
[nm]: That's your practical dedication.

326
00:21:53,309 --> 00:21:55,734
[nm]: To free myself from suffering once and for all.

327
00:21:56,620 --> 00:21:58,004
[tn]: And your body then...

328
00:21:58,380 --> 00:22:01,254
[tn]: Becomes a tool, as it always is, actually.

329
00:22:01,821 --> 00:22:08,271
[nm]: Even, like, when you are careless, fully engaged in sensuality or ill will, your body can only be a tool for your expression.

330
00:22:09,332 --> 00:22:11,640
[nm]: Well, for the belief of your expression, pretty much.

331
00:22:13,023 --> 00:22:16,686
[tn]: So, just an example, of this practical chair example.

332
00:22:16,907 --> 00:22:22,995
[tn]: So, my body's moving and I can see, I'm mindful of my eyes scanning and picking things out.

333
00:22:23,132 --> 00:22:23,892
[tn]: And, ah, there's a rock.

334
00:22:23,900 --> 00:22:25,042
[tn]: Move the rock... that's better.

335
00:22:25,480 --> 00:22:32,174
[tn]: And, within moments, a perfect chair is created without much... particular thoughts.

336
00:22:32,200 --> 00:22:40,974
[nm]: Yeah, yeah, exactly. Because you had the purpose peripherally, and that kept everything within those brackets of that significance.

337
00:22:41,273 --> 00:22:46,760
[nm]: So, whatever came, came through the brackets of practical purpose of being seated down and resting my back.

338
00:22:47,220 --> 00:22:55,376
[nm]: And the clearer the peripheral purpose theme context is, more accurately, more efficiently, you would find the corresponding thing.

339
00:22:55,380 --> 00:23:00,408
[tn]: And once those things, that sign has been met, once that goal has been met,

340
00:23:01,383 --> 00:23:03,682
[tn]: In a general sense, the action stops.

341
00:23:03,680 --> 00:23:06,928
[tn]: I don't... Now, it's like polishing stones and making look...

342
00:23:06,920 --> 00:23:08,473
[nm]: Yeah, exactly, exactly, yeah.

343
00:23:08,473 --> 00:23:09,290
[tn]: That's the point...

344
00:23:09,290 --> 00:23:10,530
[nm]: Yeah, that wasn't the point, yeah

345
00:23:10,970 --> 00:23:14,557
[nm]: You have to lose perspective in order to get in... to engage on that level.

346
00:23:15,035 --> 00:23:26,250
[nm]: And again, what would be the ultimate, one of the most fundamental contexts that is always implied in your life, in your actions, in your particular feelings, perceptions and so on?

347
00:23:26,252 --> 00:23:26,736
[nm]: It's death.

348
00:23:27,178 --> 00:23:28,362
[nm]: It's maraṇasati.

349
00:23:28,991 --> 00:23:34,797
[nm]: So, while we're sitting here talking, at the back of my mind is the context of death.

350
00:23:34,790 --> 00:23:35,490
[nm]: I will be death...

351
00:23:35,574 --> 00:23:36,164
[nm]: I will die.

352
00:23:36,995 --> 00:23:39,484
[nm]: Maybe next moment, maybe in 10 years time.

353
00:23:39,551 --> 00:23:40,088
[nm]: Doesn't matter.

354
00:23:40,094 --> 00:23:41,778
[nm]: But that context is always there.

355
00:23:42,016 --> 00:23:48,536
[nm]: So, whatever you choose to do, you have context of cessation implied in it, if you discern that context.

356
00:23:49,620 --> 00:23:53,833
[nm]: That's why you practice relinquishment, cessation, as the Buddha would encourage people.

357
00:23:54,821 --> 00:23:58,223
[nm]: Not by throwing away your particular things as, you know, see, I relinquished.

358
00:23:58,220 --> 00:23:58,629
[nm]: No.

359
00:23:58,856 --> 00:24:03,633
[nm]: Relinquishing, basically, your experience on the basis of being susceptible to death at any given time.

360
00:24:04,480 --> 00:24:06,012
[nm]: That's a context that's already in it.

361
00:24:06,469 --> 00:24:07,724
[nm]: The fundamental perspective.

362
00:24:08,210 --> 00:24:14,292
[tn]: You're talking about... because, say, I don't want to die, but you... so, as like I want a chair.

363
00:24:15,252 --> 00:24:15,950
[nm]: Yeah, well, that's what I mean. 

364
00:24:15,953 --> 00:24:17,694
[nm]: The chair was on a more particular level.

365
00:24:17,778 --> 00:24:20,090
[nm]: It was depending on your body, your needs and so on.

366
00:24:20,090 --> 00:24:23,296
[nm]: Your significance was implied by all these other things.

367
00:24:23,770 --> 00:24:30,435
[nm]: But it's the same principle, whereby you want to take it to the point of where you have no say about the context.

368
00:24:30,937 --> 00:24:32,317
[nm]: You have no say about impermanence.

369
00:24:32,310 --> 00:24:35,096
[nm]: You have no say about death, sickness, aging and death.

370
00:24:35,090 --> 00:24:36,214
[nm]: You have no say in that.

371
00:24:36,541 --> 00:24:38,469
[nm]: That's why those are the universals.

372
00:24:38,980 --> 00:24:40,292
[nm]: As the Buddha described it.

373
00:24:40,620 --> 00:24:48,557
[nm]: Things that are universal in every experience, regardless of your particular context, particular wisdom, particular sort of actions and inclinations.

374
00:24:48,580 --> 00:24:51,698
[nm]: These are things in common to all beings, basically.

375
00:24:52,590 --> 00:24:55,541
[nm]: Even if you're not aware of it, those principles remain the same.

376
00:24:55,541 --> 00:24:57,330
[nm]: That's why the Buddha didn't invent the dhamma.

377
00:24:57,578 --> 00:24:58,667
[nm]: He rediscovered it.

378
00:25:01,400 --> 00:25:05,475
[nm]: So, the context you want to be dwelling on, if you want liberation, is the context of cessation.

379
00:25:05,480 --> 00:25:06,048
[nm]: Because why?

380
00:25:06,503 --> 00:25:13,353
[nm]: Context of cessation is incompatible with maintenance of ownership of things that come on the account of your experience.

381
00:25:13,953 --> 00:25:24,911
[nm]: If your experience is liable to death, anything that comes to your experience cannot be appropriated in the same manner as if you were to forget that you are liable to death.

382
00:25:25,568 --> 00:25:33,595
[nm]: So, ownership, appropriation, this is mine, this is for me, is incompatible with I will die.

383
00:25:35,233 --> 00:25:44,627
[nm]: That's why people who engage their whole life with following the, like, ownership and desires and sensuality, are terrified at the slightest implication of death.

384
00:25:45,103 --> 00:25:48,844
[nm]: Because it fully contradicts their being that they've been nurturing and developing.

385
00:25:48,865 --> 00:25:53,376
[nm]: Being in the context of sensuality, proliferation and assumption of life.

386
00:25:54,783 --> 00:26:00,261
[nm]: So, that's why you don't have to go and undo your particular ownership if you do maraṇasati properly.

387
00:26:00,477 --> 00:26:02,280
[nm]: You will disown your very senses.

388
00:26:02,545 --> 00:26:04,570
[nm]: Yeah, because you value maraṇasati.

389
00:26:04,570 --> 00:26:06,492
[nm]: You value contemplation of death.

390
00:26:07,227 --> 00:26:09,128
[nm]: Clarification of the theme of death.

391
00:26:09,418 --> 00:26:13,650
[nm]: Inevitable... Guaranteed cessation.

392
00:26:13,974 --> 00:26:17,593
[nm]: Which means everything that's incompatible with it, you will be abandoning.

393
00:26:18,390 --> 00:26:24,915
[nm]: And that's again the simile that you heat up the pan, whatever like water falls on it, it will dry.

394
00:26:24,911 --> 00:26:27,656
[nm]: You're not getting rid of the particular drops.

395
00:26:27,652 --> 00:26:31,067
[nm]: Just maintain the heat on the pan and every... nothing can stay on it.

396
00:26:31,997 --> 00:26:32,997
[nm]: Same with fire.

397
00:26:33,100 --> 00:26:37,225
[nm]: Maintain the fire and don't worry what's thrown into the fire, because it will burn.

398
00:26:39,469 --> 00:26:43,728
[tn]: So, the level I'm talking about as well, the chair level, vertical level,

399
00:26:44,229 --> 00:26:46,978
[tn]: So, someone doesn't want to develop liberation and so on.

400
00:26:47,430 --> 00:26:51,311
[tn]: So, on a worldly level, if you want to develop anything...

401
00:26:51,469 --> 00:27:01,642
[nm]: Any skill, any wisdom, any knowledge, any recognition of the principle, any invention, yeah, requires that... requires that greater perspective, requires that stepping back.

402
00:27:02,002 --> 00:27:06,044
[nm]: Because if all you see is what's in front of you, you're never gonna see beyond it.

403
00:27:06,282 --> 00:27:10,216
[nm]: And purpose of what's in front of you is already beyond it, in a way.

404
00:27:10,818 --> 00:27:15,056
[nm]: Like, the the chair doesn't have, contain you sitting on it.

405
00:27:16,166 --> 00:27:21,162
[nm]: You being seated on the chair comfortably, it's implied when you're not seated in the chair.

406
00:27:21,947 --> 00:27:26,402
[nm]: So, the purpose of the thing you are trying to examine is absent to that thing.

407
00:27:27,892 --> 00:27:47,648
[nm]: Present, simultaneously with the thing, but present as a form of absence, because you're not actually... As you said yourself, while you were looking for the... for something to emulate a chair, to serve the purpose that you wanted, it was there in the mind and you were doing all these actions that were being directed by that purpose, by that context.

408
00:27:48,088 --> 00:27:49,410
[nm]: Once you sat, it was gone.

409
00:27:50,616 --> 00:27:51,677
[nm]: You fulfilled the purpose.

410
00:27:52,560 --> 00:27:56,313
[nm]: The mind, another thing has arisen, another mood, another direction, another context.

411
00:27:57,922 --> 00:28:01,814
[nm]: But, obviously, there was a... it was a more particular type of theme.

412
00:28:02,230 --> 00:28:13,033
[nm]: But if you develop this theme that depend on being alive, theme of impermanence, theme of maraṇasati, theme of loving-kindness, basically depends for as long as there is mind, as long as there are five aggregates.

413
00:28:13,376 --> 00:28:17,749
[nm]: Yeah, those themes will stay effortlessly for as long as the five aggregates remain.

414
00:28:19,136 --> 00:28:23,463
[tn]: Remember that you were saying about like... like geniuses.

415
00:28:23,877 --> 00:28:26,856
[tn]: So, someone's got this idea of a musical...

416
00:28:27,340 --> 00:28:35,520
[tn]: you know, a symphony. And then they have that idea, that hope that... [?in them,] and it comes out.

417
00:28:36,412 --> 00:28:42,113
[nm]: And then people who don't have the idea, they try to reverse engineer it, emulating these particular notes.

418
00:28:43,490 --> 00:28:52,524
[nm]: But the true genius or wisdom or any form of knowledge comes from that having the greater perspective maintained.

419
00:28:53,469 --> 00:28:55,391
[nm]: In regard to particular things you're doing.

420
00:28:55,768 --> 00:28:58,412
[nm]: Because often people would lose perspective once they start doing things.

421
00:28:59,031 --> 00:29:01,292
[nm]: And that's like one of the main...

422
00:29:01,850 --> 00:29:04,364
[nm]: The main characteristic of that is sensuality.

423
00:29:04,688 --> 00:29:07,244
[nm]: Sorry, that is the main characteristic of sensuality.

424
00:29:08,210 --> 00:29:12,061
[nm]: That's why sensuality is so bound with ignorance.

425
00:29:12,700 --> 00:29:17,315
[nm]: Because as soon as you engage with sensuality, you pretty much forget about your desire of sensuality.

426
00:29:17,328 --> 00:29:21,831
[nm]: Which is why also people regard sensuality as a way of satisfying your desire.

427
00:29:21,831 --> 00:29:23,715
[nm]: Which, as we discussed before, it's impossible.

428
00:29:23,711 --> 00:29:25,052
[nm]: Desire is on the level of the mind.

429
00:29:25,705 --> 00:29:27,168
[nm]: Where can sensuality be manifested?

430
00:29:27,162 --> 00:29:28,770
[nm]: On the level of body and bodily actions.

431
00:29:28,774 --> 00:29:32,110
[nm]: Which means you need magical belief that this corresponds to that.

432
00:29:32,111 --> 00:29:37,263
[nm]: As in, these actions will enable me to turn a blind eye to still present desire in my mind.

433
00:29:38,770 --> 00:29:41,694
[nm]: That's why you can never... you can never satisfy your desires.

434
00:29:41,690 --> 00:29:45,783
[nm]: And even people who never made the effort to abandon desires, knows that deep down inside.

435
00:29:46,840 --> 00:29:48,309
[nm]: Their desires cannot be satisfied.

436
00:29:48,305 --> 00:29:51,042
[nm]: By losing the sight of it and going elsewhere looking for a solution.
[tn]: You're maintaining... you're maintaining the context by...

437
00:29:51,042 --> 00:29:54,660
[nm]: By losing the sight of it and going elsewhere looking for a solution.

438
00:29:56,030 --> 00:29:57,061
[nm]: Apples and oranges.

439
00:29:57,082 --> 00:30:03,023
[tn]: So you can... so no matter how far your body goes and what it does, it maintains the context of desire for...

440
00:30:04,814 --> 00:30:09,345
[nm]: And, the more you ignore the context of desire, the more power starts to exert over you.

441
00:30:10,229 --> 00:30:13,953
[nm]: That's why the fever of sensuality grows, the more you engage with it.

442
00:30:18,722 --> 00:30:28,812
[tn]: So, that's... again, you can look at someone who's acting or doing something sensual, say, sexual intercourse, and you can know for sure that they... that background theme...

443
00:30:28,810 --> 00:30:32,840
[nm]: Everything particularly is... yeah... is fueled by the background theme.

444
00:30:32,910 --> 00:30:34,242
[nm]: You remove the background theme,

445
00:30:34,583 --> 00:30:38,157
[nm]: all these particular things become meaningless, even quite repulsive.

446
00:30:39,107 --> 00:30:39,936
[nm]: Same with food.

447
00:30:39,936 --> 00:30:47,941
[nm]: Like, if you're really, really hungry, hunger overwhelmed your mind, and then you're just shoving things down your mouth, just like a wild animal.

448
00:30:49,511 --> 00:30:55,720
[nm]: Because you're so... you have... you've completely lost the sight of the context, but the context is so powerful influencing your behavior.

449
00:30:57,288 --> 00:31:03,494
[nm]: That's why you want to be unduly in the contexts, the themes of lust, the themes of ill-will, resistance,

450
00:31:03,660 --> 00:31:05,612
[nm]: The themes of carelessness, distraction.

451
00:31:06,490 --> 00:31:08,391
[nm]: And you can't undo them mechanically.

452
00:31:08,947 --> 00:31:16,856
[nm]: Oh, if you walk like this, talk like this, sit like this, everything, any unwholesome theme will be uprooted from you.

453
00:31:16,850 --> 00:31:17,189
[nm]: No.

454
00:31:17,360 --> 00:31:18,309
[nm]: How can that be?

455
00:31:18,494 --> 00:31:25,730
[nm]: Because all of those efforts are aimed at the particular modes of behavior that are already secondary to any theme that's presently enduring.

456
00:31:27,202 --> 00:31:34,448
[nm]: But if I teach you how to discern the theme, and you see it for yourself, you don't need to worry about how you sit, how you walk, how you talk.

457
00:31:35,669 --> 00:31:38,650
[nm]: Because you uprooted the basis of desire for those manifestations.

458
00:31:38,650 --> 00:31:40,427
[tn]: The mind is dragging everything with it.

459
00:31:40,572 --> 00:31:41,860
[nm]: Yeah, yeah.

460
00:31:43,240 --> 00:31:45,566
[tn]: To make sure that that vehicle [?is clean].

461
00:31:45,692 --> 00:31:52,970
[nm]: See, when I say you have to discern the theme in order to develop it, one of the prerequisites for developing the right view is discern the mind.

462
00:31:53,368 --> 00:31:58,974
[nm]: Which means most people, puthujjanas, can't even discern the mind as a phenomenon, as a basic context.

463
00:31:59,772 --> 00:32:01,334
[nm]: All they can see is what's in front of it.

464
00:32:01,709 --> 00:32:08,027
[nm]: Yet, whatever's in front of it, it's meaningful because of the peripheral, because of that background, because of that context.

465
00:32:08,210 --> 00:32:11,378
[nm]: But that's completely, completely invisible to them.

466
00:32:14,837 --> 00:32:23,029
[nm]: Yeah, so that's why I said, like, if you start... if you... if you stop investing so much effort in conceptualizing, in trying to, like,

467
00:32:23,520 --> 00:32:33,830
[nm]: informationally, sort of, clarify out of this, this particular movements and what you should do, and start looking at the context of the very action of you conceptualizing things, right there and then.

468
00:32:33,831 --> 00:32:36,553
[nm]: That's why, like, come back to the present moment.

469
00:32:37,347 --> 00:32:46,991
[nm]: Don't just come back to the mystical, vague present moment, but discern the present context that takes you away from the present moment, as in, distracts you from it.

470
00:32:46,995 --> 00:32:47,372
[nm]: You can't.

471
00:32:47,370 --> 00:32:49,686
[nm]: You are in the present moment, whether you want to be or not.

472
00:32:49,865 --> 00:32:50,673
[nm]: It's not optional.

473
00:32:51,637 --> 00:32:56,147
[nm]: So, you want to discern the context that endures then, regardless of where your mind goes.

474
00:32:56,223 --> 00:32:58,168
[nm]: You want to find universal basis for it.

475
00:33:01,122 --> 00:33:02,385
[tn]: It also frees you from it.

476
00:33:02,844 --> 00:33:03,092
[nm]: Yeah.

477
00:33:03,092 --> 00:33:12,149
[nm]: So, you... you... you... and also it kind of... it's... it's more ambiguous, it's harder to discern, but actually, in the long run, it's easier to do.

478
00:33:13,071 --> 00:33:19,572
[nm]: Because it's not as futile as just chasing concepts and staying with, like, particularized aspects of things.

479
00:33:20,040 --> 00:33:27,204
[nm]: Oh, I'm breathing in like this, I'm breathing in like this, I'm moving in like this, I speak slowly, I do this, I'm controlling, I'm controlling, I'm controlling.

480
00:33:27,726 --> 00:33:27,860
[nm]: No.

481
00:33:27,861 --> 00:33:28,808
[nm]: Look at the context.

482
00:33:29,380 --> 00:33:31,667
[nm]: Look at the significance of all these particular acts.

483
00:33:32,240 --> 00:33:35,684
[nm]: You become aware of that, you're automatically aware of all these particular things.

484
00:33:36,745 --> 00:33:44,566
[nm]: So, if you're aware of your... if you don't lose the context, for example, that gives reason to you eating food today,

485
00:33:46,846 --> 00:33:50,008
[nm]: means you won't be eating food carelessly.

486
00:33:50,610 --> 00:33:53,627
[nm]: What is the context for eating food in your present situation?

487
00:33:54,343 --> 00:33:59,911
[nm]: Oh, it's for sustenance of the body, removing the hunger, but not overdoing it, not giving rise to sensuality.

488
00:33:59,913 --> 00:34:00,701
[nm]: That's the context.

489
00:34:01,100 --> 00:34:13,547
[nm]: But if you think that context will be found in you chewing food like three times per ten seconds, and then I will swallow slowly, and then I will look up when I put food in my mouth, so I don't see what I'm putting in my mouth.

490
00:34:13,991 --> 00:34:15,745
[nm]: All that means you don't know where the problem is.

491
00:34:15,780 --> 00:34:18,541
[nm]: The problem is not how you eat the food or what you eat.

492
00:34:18,762 --> 00:34:26,713
[nm]: The problem is your reason for eating is rooted at... in lust or aversion or whatever, delusion or all of it.

493
00:34:28,110 --> 00:34:34,606
[nm]: But if you maintain the context, the reason why you eat food, you know exactly when to stop and you wouldn't care what you eat.

494
00:34:34,960 --> 00:34:36,328
[nm]: If it's agreeable, agreeable.

495
00:34:36,322 --> 00:34:38,962
[nm]: If it's disagreeable, you won't be sad about it either.

496
00:34:40,328 --> 00:34:43,347
[tn]: It's like it's free from duty and virtue.

497
00:34:43,543 --> 00:34:45,240
[nm]: Sure, sure, yeah.

498
00:34:45,242 --> 00:34:46,185
[tn]: You go beyond that.

499
00:34:46,187 --> 00:34:46,873
[nm]: Go beyond that.

500
00:34:47,270 --> 00:34:50,981
[nm]: That's like the first fetter that falls off, once you start developing context properly.

501
00:34:52,070 --> 00:34:55,374
[nm]: Like, I must be doing this. No, you must...

502
00:34:55,380 --> 00:34:57,370
[nm]: There's nothing you must be doing, in a way.

503
00:34:58,545 --> 00:35:04,294
[nm]: Because if there are certain things you must be doing, that will automatically result in nibbāna, then the Buddha would have said so.

504
00:35:04,867 --> 00:35:08,960
[nm]: He would have said that action, particular action, will result in liberation.

505
00:35:08,964 --> 00:35:09,269
[nm]: But no.

506
00:35:09,261 --> 00:35:11,640
[nm]: He said that any action results in more action.

507
00:35:12,530 --> 00:35:15,511
[nm]: Good action is better than bad action, but it's still action.

508
00:35:15,970 --> 00:35:20,075
[nm]: Which means you can't step outside of the domain of action while you keep performing action.

509
00:35:21,551 --> 00:35:30,500
[nm]: So, first you replace bad action with good action, but then you want to develop the universal context, theme, of non-lust, non-aversion, non-distraction towards any action.

510
00:35:30,505 --> 00:35:36,482
[nm]: And that's how you free yourself from virtue and duty, from expression of lust, aversion, delusion, all of it.

511
00:35:36,590 --> 00:35:37,665
[nm]: That's how you become an arahant.

512
00:35:38,330 --> 00:35:43,846
[nm]: Arahant has the ultimate imperturbable context of impermanence that has saturated him thoroughly.

513
00:35:43,974 --> 00:35:48,804
[nm]: There is nothing that he can ever appropriate again, regard as mine.

514
00:35:49,214 --> 00:35:49,520
[nm]: Mine.

515
00:35:50,861 --> 00:35:56,233
[nm]: You want to develop the principle and you will see it working because you will be unable to suffer amid suffering.

516
00:35:59,713 --> 00:36:02,429
[nm]: So, how can you not suffer amidst the particular suffering?

517
00:36:03,225 --> 00:36:07,374
[nm]: Because it's the context that gives suffering, not particular things that give suffering to the context.

518
00:36:08,528 --> 00:36:13,427
[nm]: If suffering were truly coming from external things, particular things, you wouldn't be able to free yourself from it.

519
00:36:14,120 --> 00:36:17,806
[nm]: That's why Arahant, who has freed himself fundamentally from the context of suffering,

520
00:36:18,830 --> 00:36:26,595
[nm]: he says: oh, the only disturbance, disturbance that is left is on account of these five aggregates, but even that will go when I... when the body breaks apart.

521
00:36:27,088 --> 00:36:29,370
[nm]: So, he doesn't refer to only suffering that's left.

522
00:36:29,370 --> 00:36:30,753
[nm]: No, it's disturbance that's left.

523
00:36:30,755 --> 00:36:33,012
[nm]: All this annoyance that's left is on the...

524
00:36:33,010 --> 00:36:41,010
[nm]: But, for Putujana, annoyance is suffering, because he hasn't developed the context that frees him from suffering, fundamentally.

525
00:36:41,294 --> 00:36:48,004
[nm]: And then, he can't suffer amidst all these things that used to... that used to, kind of, reverse, so to speak,

526
00:36:48,920 --> 00:37:13,254
[nm]: that order, whereby, instead of realizing the suffering comes from your mind, that's where you... that's... it's your craving in your mind, in regard to the circumstances, that's making you suffer, not having that perspective, not having the four noble truths, you would then think that these particular things, that's why you suffer, that's why there is suffering in my mind, because this happened that I didn't want to happen.

527
00:37:14,380 --> 00:37:17,128
[nm]: Whatever happens, that's not the root for suffering.

528
00:37:17,150 --> 00:37:20,503
[nm]: The root of suffering is craving in your mind in regard to whatever happens.

529
00:37:21,063 --> 00:37:24,873
[nm]: And craving, in a way, it's already a form of context that people are unaware of.

530
00:37:26,631 --> 00:37:35,233
[nm]: You are unaware of that it's you who induce the suffering by wanting more of pleasure or less of displeasure, or wanting to just ignore the neutral.

531
00:37:36,340 --> 00:37:37,621
[nm]: That's on the level of the mind.

532
00:37:40,820 --> 00:37:52,626
[tn]: So, a person who wants to get the idea: I want to be free from suffering. So, getting that idea, what is, you know, is it craving [?important, what i its supposed to be like...]

533
00:37:52,714 --> 00:37:58,445
[nm]: Yeah. Obviously, you need, for such a universal context, you need Buddha's instruction.

534
00:37:58,450 --> 00:38:03,797
[nm]: Because if you find your way out of suffering by yourself, well, you would be either Sammāsambuddha or Paccekabuddha.

535
00:38:05,816 --> 00:38:09,296
[nm]: But good news is, you don't have to find it by yourself, because dhamma is still out there.

536
00:38:11,013 --> 00:38:12,434
[nm]: But you want to see that suffering,

537
00:38:12,917 --> 00:38:16,320
[nm]: doesn't... Nobody says you deserve what comes your way in terms of circumstances.

538
00:38:16,328 --> 00:38:16,592
[nm]: No.

539
00:38:17,498 --> 00:38:18,400
[nm]: It has nothing to do with it.

540
00:38:19,580 --> 00:38:23,882
[nm]: But you suffering on account of whatever comes your way in regard to circumstances, that's on you.

541
00:38:25,205 --> 00:38:26,493
[nm]: Suffering is your own doing.

542
00:38:26,493 --> 00:38:28,466
[nm]: And because of that, you can actually free yourself.

543
00:38:29,813 --> 00:38:35,464
[nm]: To the point where the Buddha said himself, even if the highway robbers pin you down and start sawing your limbs one by one savagely,

544
00:38:36,024 --> 00:38:39,104
[nm]: you won't have ill will towards them, because you won't be suffering on account of it.

545
00:38:39,270 --> 00:38:50,866
[nm]: It's going to be extreme disturbance, extremely disagreeable, but not... it cannot reverse that order and take over the context of the mind that has been liberated from it.

546
00:38:52,621 --> 00:38:53,338
[nm]: It's impossible.

547
00:38:54,510 --> 00:38:55,464
[tn]: The pressure...

548
00:38:56,386 --> 00:39:02,786
[nm]: No amount of pressure of disagreeable can become... can induce craving to get rid of it, because craving always comes from the mind.

549
00:39:09,640 --> 00:39:13,368
[nm]: And if you have relinquished the ownership of that mind, what is there to resist?

550
00:39:13,380 --> 00:39:14,504
[nm]: What is there to defend?

551
00:39:16,230 --> 00:39:17,632
[nm]: What is there to crave against?

552
00:39:18,346 --> 00:39:18,850
[nm]: Nothing.

